/*!
@page algea-impl ALGEA

@section algea_matrix ALGEA_MATRIX data structure
ALGEA_MATRIX contains a one dimensional array of coordinates, paired with a @p row and @p column attribute. The two dimensional behavior is then implemented using the ALGEAat() function. The reason for this choice is that it is both simpler to work with than the alternative, which is a array of pointers to rows, and it is faster. Indeed, allocation only requires one call to @p malloc(), and traversing the array can exploit spatial locality with the cache (see <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Locality_of_reference#">Locality of reference</a>, for example). It does decrease the maximum number of elements compared to an array of pointers to rows, as the whole array's size must fit in a @p size_t, however this is irrelevant in the applications this library will see (assuming the elements are @p double, taking up 8 bytes, and @p SIZE_MAX being @f$2^{64}@f$, then we can have at most @f$2^{18}@f$ elements). Also, this means that the shape of the matrix cannot influence how many items can be stored, which is desirable behavior. It is also less convenient for the end user, as the array index notation cannot be used, but that is a necessary tradeoff. The choice was also made to not support views, which is to say that the assumption is that the array of elements is only accessed through that ALGEA_MATRIX. Reshaping is also not supported (@p rows and @p columns are thus @p const qualified).

@section matrix_multiplication Matrix Multiplication
The loop order chosen is @p i, @p k, @p j. The reason for this is that as the matrices are row major, the rightmost index varies fastest, which is to say that neighbor column indices are neighbors in memory (for the same row, of course). As @p j is always the rightmost index, it should be in the inner loop to take advantage of the cache, then @p k appears once as the column index, and @p i never does, @p i should be in the outer loop.

Special care also needed when the result matrix overlaps with one of the arguments, as otherwise the factors will vary during the operation. As it is not supported to access the memory otherwise than through the owner matrix, it is sufficient to verify the ALGEA_MATRIX pointers do not overlap. If they do, the repeated matrix can simply be copied. Only one copy is ever made, as if both matrices overlap with result, then they are the same. Then, the routine where the result is separate can then simply be applied with the copied matrix.
*/
